Tree edit distance. The Tree edit distance (TED) algorithm calculates the number of steps required to transform one tree into
another. Traditionally, the TED algorithm counts the costs of three
operations: insert, delete, and replace. In our current implementation, we adopt a simplified approach. We calculate the differences
in depths for each node, which means we only consider the insert
and delete operations. We do not compare the content of each
node during this process. By focusing solely on the differences in
depths and considering only insert and delete operations, our
implementation provides a straightforward and efficient way to
measure the similarity between two trees.
