ICL4D-R. As shown in Table 4, ICL4D-R consistently achieves
the highest re-executability rate across all compiler optimization
levels (O0–O3) and both datasets. On HumanEval-Decompile, it
reaches 54.3% at O0, outperforming DeepSeek-V3.2 (46.7%) and
LLM4Decompile (26.8%). A similar pattern holds on ExeBench, where
ICL4D-R achieves up to 20% absolute improvement. The consistent advantage across optimization levels indicates that retrievalbased contextual examples help the model generalize to diverse
compiler transformations, yielding both syntactically valid and
semantically faithful reconstructions.
ICL4D-O. The rule-based variant, ICL4D-O, explicitly incorporates compiler optimization flags into the prompt. Since decompilers
tend to fail more frequently under higher optimization levels, we
apply ICL4D-O specifically to these first-round failures from O1–O3
assemblies. Because LLM-based decompilation exhibits inherent
stochasticity—where the same assembly may succeed or fail across
different runs, we adopt a controlled baseline that re-feeds previously failed samples for a second decompilation attempt. As shown
in Table 5, this targeted setting yields selective gains—for instance,
-ftree-coalesce-vars at O2 improves executability to 17.35% on
HumanEval—though overall stability remains limited due to the
rigidity of rule-based prompting.
Despite this, manual inspection reveals that ICL4D-O’s failures
are typically localized and easier to diagnose, rather than causing
widespread semantic corruption. We further analyze this in Section 5.2, where we show that even when executability is comparable,
ICL4D-O tends to generate code that is easier to fix.
Answer to RQ1. In-context learning improves the executability of decompiled code. ICL4D-R achieves the most consistent
and significant gains across datasets and optimization levels.
ICL4D-O is less stable but introduces more structured, repairable errors, a property further examined in RQ2.

5.2

RQ2: Error Mitigation

To answer the question of whether in-context learning can mitigate
specific categories of decompilation errors, we performed a finegrained analysis of both compilation and runtime failures across
datasets and optimization levels. While overall re-executability
captures the aggregate success rate, it does not reveal which kinds
of reasoning errors are corrected by the model. By decomposing
failures into interpretable categories, we can assess how in-context
learning improves structural reconstruction, semantic consistency,
and runtime correctness.
Specifically, we analyzed the stderr output of all failed compilations and executions and mapped each message to error categories
based on compiler diagnostic patterns. This taxonomy enables us to

