To assess robustness across program complexity, we stratify functions by cyclomatic complexity and lines of code, as shown in
Figure 4. Our ICL4D-R method consistently outperforms the baseline (a general LLM without contextual guidance) across all bins on
both HumanEval-Decompile (top) and ExeBench (bottom) datasets.
Although performance declines as complexity and code length
increase, the degradation is notably slower for our approach, indicating stronger generalization to structurally and semantically
intricate functions. In particular, the advantage is most pronounced
in the mid-complexity and mid-size ranges (e.g., 5–10 branches or
15–30 lines), suggesting that in-context guidance helps the model
preserve control-flow and data-flow coherence even as program
structure grows more elaborate. We do not evaluate ICL4D-O for
RQ3, as for rule-based prompts, higher program complexity does
not necessarily imply greater difficulty—performance instead depends on whether the provided optimization rule is relevant to the
optimization flags present in the target assembly.

