as shown in Figure 3. The original source code correctly parses
up to nine digit characters from a string, converts them into a
decimal integer stored in *addr, and returns the number of characters processed. The ICL4D-R successfully reconstructs this control
flow and arithmetic structure, including initialization, loop bounds,
and incremental parsing logic. By contrast, DeepSeek-V3.2 degenerates into a single-character digit check and returns a constant
Boolean value, omitting the numeric accumulation and loop entirely. LLM4Decompile-End-1.3B introduces spurious control
flow and bitwise operations (e.g., get(&s), c &= ~0xff), producing
code that is syntactically valid but semantically unrelated to the
intended decimal conversion. Overall, the figure illustrates that
retrieval-augmented prompting (ICL4D-R) better preserves program semantics, while purely generative models often suffer from
control-flow collapse or semantic hallucination.
Answer to RQ2. In-context learning mitigates structural errors in decompilation. ICL4D-R effectively reduces syntax and
declaration failures, improving structural and symbolic consistency, while ICL4D-O produces more localized and repairable
errors.

