5.4

Ablation Studies

To verify that the performance improvement originates from semantically relevant retrieval rather than merely increased context
length, we introduce a random retrieval control condition. In this
variant, the retrieved in-context examples are replaced with randomly sampled functions from the same corpus, while keeping the
total context length identical to that of the retrieval-augmented
setting. All other components remain unchanged.
Table 7 reports the re-executability rates across the baseline,
retrieval, and random-retrieval conditions. We expect that random
retrieval will significantly reduce re-execution rates and shift the
error distribution toward syntax and other categories, demonstrating that semantically aligned retrieval contexts provide meaningful
guidance for structure reconstruction and variable recovery.

