This suggests that Manifold’s rule-based lifting captures general
properties of the x86-64 compilation process—stack discipline, calling conventions, common instruction selection patterns—rather
than compiler-specific idioms. The result is practically significant:
analysts rarely know which compiler produced a given binary, and
a decompiler whose output quality is sensitive to that choice would
impose an additional burden on the reverse engineering workflow.

