Case Study: Calling Convention Recovery. In PE binaries,
Microsoft’s x64 conventions enforces that the first, second,
and third integer arguments of a function are passed via registers rcx, rdx, and r8, respectively. Yet for ELF and Mach-O,
AMD64’s System V ABI instead passes these via rdi, rsi,
and rdx, respectively. Shown in Figure 6, Reko’s call recovery
succeeds on PE binaries, yet fails on non-PE binaries’ conventions, resulting in incorrect argument ordering. Ensuring
decompilers’ accuracy with these subtle differences reinforces
the need to explore both ELF and non-ELF formats.
