stage with the no option stack structure. 22 tests failed there, with the primary reason
being that later P-Code and LLVM IR instructions attempt to index or access those data
structures in ways that do not make sense. For instance, the data may be accessed under
the assumption that sequentially defined data is arranged in the same order in memory.
Those types of accesses are only valid in both the byte addressable and single struct
stack structures. The second interesting finding is that the single struct stack structure
has the best success rate, while byte addressable is actually the worst. The improvement
in lifting failures for the former translates to an improved overall success rate making it
the best option for future decompiling lifter designs.
